full than in recent years. Officials say supplies are secure, but analysts warn that a cold winter or import disruptions could push prices higher. So how secure is the supply?</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's car crisis: Can the 35-hour workweek survive?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6d6e757bf782ffca632557df20d628d63149a19c695ddad2e3141e77cb0bf617</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T08:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-car-crisis-can-the-35-hour-workweek-survive/a-79307488--cb2e3220b33e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-car-crisis-can-the-35-hour-workweek-survive/a-79307488?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German carmakers want employees to work 40 hours instead of the current 35 without extra pay, arguing it would improve competitiveness. Unions strongly oppose the proposal and view it as an attack on workers' rights.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Germany faces energy tax losses as EV sales rise</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ca35e5baaac83a66d2e209b5802ed01c130f1fb2c2bc13badd99f0c3cae113dd</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-20T14:02:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-faces-energy-tax-losses-as-ev-sales-rise/a-79320422--82b05ee44dbb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-faces-energy-tax-losses-as-ev-sales-rise/a-79320422?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The rapid shift to electric vehicles in Germany is cutting fuel tax revenue for the government. As EV sales surge, policymakers face growing pressure to find new ways to fund roads and transportation infrastructure.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Global Gender Gap Report: Women's hard-won gains 'fragile'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:10acfae979a29c3c010178eabf4639a889d554dd6559c28775a2fdeb720c518a</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T10:05:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/global-gender-gap-report-women-s-hard-won-gains-fragile/a-79280086--9ed95bd98a77.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/global-gender-gap-report-women-s-hard-won-gains-fragile/a-79280086?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Two decades of progress toward gender parity is at risk as women's political representation declines, according to a new World Economic Forum report. At the current pace, the remaining gap could take 120 years to close.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Why the era of cheap government debt is over</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5bb382755b1f543dd6c840c6fb295852e2c35b286991cabb0ddb1fed62bc4aa3</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T13:17:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-the-era-of-cheap-government-debt-is-over/a-79259272--435550125c87.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-the-era-of-cheap-government-debt-is-over/a-79259272?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">US debt has topped $40 trillion. How risky is the country's growing debt burden. And will investors keep funding Washington's deficits?</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Why AI researchers warn humanity could face extinction</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c4400907c5ffc69f59dc40ceca35810d8546a651a576a9a8e28fea1979574480</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T08:15:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-ai-researchers-warn-humanity-could-face-extinction/a-79263146--d42b075a07d3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-ai-researchers-warn-humanity-could-face-extinction/a-79263146?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Could artificial intelligence pose an existential threat to humanity? Some leading researchers are warning against an unregulated race toward AI superintelligence.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Africa's richest man Dangote's IPO out of reach in Nigeria</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:217fd91ffb653ac5bf8914e0e01d1775417d9e8f3a9ccbfa3deb7f5a1db00c6d</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T14:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/africa-s-richest-man-dangote-s-ipo-out-of-reach-in-nigeria/a-79263398--03e0d1756308.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/africa-s-richest-man-dangote-s-ipo-out-of-reach-in-nigeria/a-79263398?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Dangote seeks $1.6 billion from investors to finance a $14.3 billion expansion and thus double the oil refinery's capacity. The offer could give millions of Nigerians a stake in the company. But who can truly afford it?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Can Iran replace sea trade with land routes?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e165cb2864a05d6858466ee66b24db7fafbad90fa01833d237589e1645427075</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T07:50:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/can-iran-replace-sea-trade-with-land-routes/a-79211179--9748aa6cdc36.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-iran-replace-sea-trade-with-land-routes/a-79211179?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Iran is shifting trade toward northern ports and land routes as its southern shipping faces a US blockade. Analysts say the corridors can keep essential goods moving, but cannot replace maritime trade.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Why are European banks moving gold out of United States?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d77bc4a01127962a13e51b5c781afb0e0bd8eea4fd21a4aa0a070193c949568d</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-12T15:32:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-are-european-banks-moving-gold-out-of-united-states/a-79213013--320ff1551fa3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-are-european-banks-moving-gold-out-of-united-states/a-79213013?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Netherlands and France have moved their gold out of US vaults. Part of the reason is fears about the reliability of the US, but equally important is the need to keep gold closer to home in the event of a crisis.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>India looks to steer BRICS away from anti-Western turn</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6561eaec654949e979a525d37644a0d86f3f5133d147b34952071a6ca8963c43</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-11T10:55:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/india-looks-to-steer-brics-away-from-anti-western-turn/a-79195234--e9a076e9f28e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/india-looks-to-steer-brics-away-from-anti-western-turn/a-79195234?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The BRICS summit 2026 takes place in Delhi this weekend. Unlike last year, Putin and Xi are expected to attend. Can India keep the expanded bloc united?</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: Is Saxony-Anhalt's economy really struggling?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:64e23ca6cda00144578c9c21a47b5c8a35e0cbd118e68364b744a6eaaef4bd69</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T10:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-is-saxony-anhalt-s-economy-really-struggling/a-78867143--acfa7a18352e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-is-saxony-anhalt-s-economy-really-struggling/a-78867143?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Germany's far-right AfD party has long claimed that the eastern state of Saxony-Anhalt has been left behind. Is that really the case? A closer look at the data offers some interesting insights.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>How Volkswagen's huge workforce became a costly burden</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:665f2bd5a40f13cfe05401d29a1fe16950010113523c6dfea662b1e5d1786868</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T07:21:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-volkswagen-s-huge-workforce-became-a-costly-burden/a-77766154--6b1e762393dc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-volkswagen-s-huge-workforce-became-a-costly-burden/a-77766154?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The struggling German carmaker plans to cut up to 100,000 jobs from its 630,000-strong workforce. How did VW's headcount get so bloated, and will the cuts be enough to survive the Chinese EV onslaught?</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Is Germany's economic slump finally over?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3518d4c7c92cf73fe3abe12547166f4ab59974b415fb6a08792f95c1384ec744</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T12:30:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/is-germany-s-economic-slump-finally-over/a-78815447--a101d614d4d2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/is-germany-s-economic-slump-finally-over/a-78815447?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Despite a slew of negative headlines, the German economy has posted its best figures in years. There are reasons for optimism, but structural issues remain.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Can new Apple CEO John Ternus fix the tech giant's AI woes?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c288d0026418f6836c9d229c137c4ec6f62a8fcca6f5a094d0b6a685da640887</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T09:49:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/can-new-apple-ceo-john-ternus-fix-the-tech-giant-s-ai-woes/a-78575898--ec9f6d3fb1f9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/can-new-apple-ceo-john-ternus-fix-the-tech-giant-s-ai-woes/a-78575898?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">John Ternus is just the third Apple CEO in 30 years. His primary challenge is to correct the company's faltering AI strategy to preserve its position as a big tech leader.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Canada's Carney holds firm against Trump tariffs</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:54f789a2dc785f3d7731c59bf8015fdda2aabbc3da26a00fd6e2400b9d9d2084</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-08-31T13:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/canada-s-carney-holds-firm-against-trump-tariffs/a-78542754--4ad6d16c6985.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/canada-s-carney-holds-firm-against-trump-tariffs/a-78542754?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Canada is facing new US import penalties after Prime Minister Mark Carney refused to match US tariffs on China. Other countries are watching as the Trump trade clash turns into a fight over economic sovereignty.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>The great lake divide: Collapse vs. comeback</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a3dd24ca346144c17c41a8b82c4e9d30326dd5ac77f29191d2ff01faccc7445d</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-08-27T09:38:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-great-lake-divide-collapse-vs-comeback/a-78460263--04bbfa728d70.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-great-lake-divide-collapse-vs-comeback/a-78460263?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As some of the world's biggest lakes vanish in real time, others are proving that recovery is still within reach.</summary><category term="Environment" /></entry><entry><title>Why social media addiction is so hard to regulate</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ee14284bc335357f6c017a0eafe29b3a2aa8026c31ce1676a5d23d47fe079fad</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-08-27T09:25:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-social-media-addiction-is-so-hard-to-regulate/a-78482023--933d64d6576a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-social-media-addiction-is-so-hard-to-regulate/a-78482023?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Facebook, Instagram and other social media platforms are accused of deliberately fostering addictive behavior among young users. Yet regulators continue to struggle to rein in their engagement-driven business models.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Gamescom 2026: What's next for the gaming industry?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:38588314b58206b57a7bce4b0ca58d3fa903b59874f56614535c5da15f327cd1</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-08-26T11:15:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/gamescom-2026-what-s-next-for-the-gaming-industry/a-78502849--a4d043f1bdf6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/gamescom-2026-what-s-next-for-the-gaming-industry/a-78502849?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As Gamescom 2026 opens in Cologne, AI, hardware costs and slowing user growth are putting pressure on the global games industry despite its current strength.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Why Germany's Volkswagen could cut 100,000 jobs</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:802335423514a86697724979d14772d2d5016bbc37b4d87167c49bdf5b5fb0cb</id><updated>2026-09-25T13:57:15Z</updated><published>2026-08-25T13:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-germany-s-volkswagen-could-cut-100-000-jobs/a-78483715--3ad4df2f83bb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-germany-s-volkswagen-could-cut-100-000-jobs/a-78483715?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Europe's biggest automaker, Volkswagen, faces Chinese competition, high costs and US tariffs. What could its restructuring mean for the German car industry?</summary><category term="Business" /></entry></feed>
